# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DO NOT try to block dev files here. Measured 2026-08-11: Cloudflare Pages
# serves an existing static asset in preference to any _redirects rule, so a
# "/README.md /404.html 404" line does nothing. Proven with a probe rule on a
# path with no file behind it — the probe returned 301 (so the file was live)
# while /README.md kept returning 200 with real content.
#
# Pages publishes everything committed, which exposed /_dev/e2e/*, /README.md,
# /package.json, /package-lock.json, /.github/workflows/e2e.yml and
# /index-maintenance. No credentials were in any of them — every secret
# resolves from process.env or GitHub Actions — but the E2E suite maps the CRM
# API surface and names the cleanup endpoint's X-E2E-Secret header.
#
# They cannot be .gitignored either, and this is the key point: the e2e
# workflow file IS read from the repo by GitHub, `npm ci` needs
# package-lock.json, and `npm run test:e2e` runs `node _dev/e2e/run.js`.
# .gitignore controls what is in the repo; the build command controls what is
# on the CDN. Only the second question is the one we need answered.
#
# FIXED 2026-08-12 by a Cloudflare Pages BUILD COMMAND that strips them from
# the deployed output:
#   rm -rf _dev .github partials README.md package.json package-lock.json index-maintenance.html
# It names `partials` too, which is now a harmless no-op — that directory was
# deleted from the repo outright (dead code: js/load-header.js fetched
# partials/navigation.html, but no page ever included load-header.js).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

